AAC-LC fixtures, made with ffmpeg 9.0.2 (its native `aac` encoder, LGPL-2.1+,
from synthetic signals generated by lavfi: nothing of anyone's recording).
Each is ADTS (`-f adts`), one second long.

  S=aevalsrc=exprs=0.4*sin(2*PI*440*t)+0.3*sin(2*PI*3000*t)*lt(mod(t\,0.25)\,0.01)+0.05*random(0):d=1:s=44100:c=stereo
  st44.aac          ffmpeg -f lavfi -i "$S" -c:a aac -b:a 96k -f adts
  st44-tools.aac    the same with -aac_pns 1 -aac_tns 1 -aac_is 1 -aac_ms 1
  mono22.aac        440 Hz sine plus noise, mono, 22050 Hz, -b:a 32k
  surround51.aac    six sines and noise, 5.1, 48000 Hz, -b:a 256k
  mono8.aac         440 Hz sine plus noise, mono, 8000 Hz, -b:a 16k

The transients in st44 make the encoder use eight short windows; -aac_pns and
-aac_is make it use noise substitution and intensity stereo; M/S, TNS and pulses
appear as the encoder finds them useful (aac_fixture_test.go logs the counts).

The fuzz seeds in ../fuzz/FuzzAAC* are frames and headers cut from these files,
and a few AudioSpecificConfig byte strings.

ref/ holds what ffmpeg 9.0.2 (Homebrew, arm64, Apple clang 21) decodes them to with
its C code paths, as float32 little-endian, interleaved (the oracle of TestAACOracle):

  ffmpeg -cpuflags 0 -i st44.aac -f f32le ref/st44.f32           (likewise st44-tools,
  mono22, mono8)
  ffmpeg -cpuflags 0 -i surround51.aac -frames:a 12 -f f32le ref/surround51.f32

HE-AAC fixtures, made with macOS `afconvert -f m4af -d aach -b <rate>` (Apple's
encoder) from synthetic lavfi sines (a 440 Hz tone, a slowly modulated 2.5 kHz
tone, an amplitude-modulated 9 kHz tone, two channels), 2 seconds, then
`ffmpeg -c:a copy -f adts`. The ADTS header carries the CORE rate and the AAC-LC
profile; the SBR data is in the fill elements.

  he44-st.aac       stereo, core 22050 Hz (SBR output 44100), -b 48000
  he32-st.aac       stereo, core 16000 Hz (SBR output 32000), -b 40000
  he48-mono.aac     mono, core 24000 Hz (SBR output 48000), -b 24000. No PS data in it:
                    ffmpeg calls it "HE-AACv2" and decodes it to stereo because an
                    implicit-signalled mono SBR stream might carry PS
  he44-ps.aac       HE-AAC v2 (`-d aacp -b 32000`) from a stereo source: mono core
                    22050 Hz, SBR and Parametric Stereo data in every frame

ref/he44-st.f32 and ref-linux/he44-st.f32 are what ffmpeg 9.0.2 decodes he44-st.aac
to (its first 24 frames of 2048 samples per channel, 44100 Hz stereo, SBR included):
macOS clang as above, and the linux gcc 14 build of ffaac (`build-info.txt` says how
it was configured; C code paths only, `--disable-asm`), run with
`ffmpeg -cpuflags 0 -i he44-st.aac -frames:a 24 -f f32le he44-st.f32`. The same for
he32-st.aac and he48-mono.aac with `-frames:a 16` (he48-mono comes out stereo, both
channels the same: ffmpeg treats an implicit-signalled mono SBR stream as possible
Parametric Stereo; the tests read channel 0). They are compared by
aac_sbr_ffmpeg_test.go (low band) and aac_sbr_adj_test.go (the whole output, with the
envelope adjustment).
